DZIP(1)                        ZOAU Command Syntax                       DZIP(1)

NAME

    dzip - Dump and terse a set of datasets to an archive file or dataset.

SYNOPSIS

    dzip [OPTIONS] [<FILE>|<DATASET>] [<DATASET-PATTERN>|<VOLUME>]+ [<VOLUME>]

    You can specify some options with either a short or long flag. If an option
    also requires an argument, you must specify the argument along with your
    chosen flag type.
    For example: -e <DATASET_PATTERN> or --exclude=<DATASET_PATTERN>

    --compress, --no-compress
        Compress the dataset or file produced by ADRDSSU DUMP. This option can
        reduce the size of the temporary dataset produced before it is passed
        to AMATERSE for tersing. It can also reduce the overall time it takes
        to run the dzip command. Enabling this option with --terse may increase
        the size of the dzip file or dataset. Equivalent to the
        ZCOMPRESS(PREF) COMPRESS command keywords.

        Defaults to --no-compress.

    -d, --debug
        Print out debug messages.

    -D, --dataset
        Dump to a z/OS dataset instead of a z/OS UNIX file.

    -e, --exclude=<DATASET_PATTERN>
        Exclude particular dataset patterns when dumping the source datasets.
        This option is ignored if dumping a volume. Equivalent to the EXCLUDE
        command keyword.

    -f, --force, --tolerate
        Specifies that target datasets are to be processed even though shared
        or exclusive access fails. Also specifies that processing continues
        if I/O errors occur during read operations.
        Equivalent to the TOL(ENQF, IOER) command keyword.

    -h, --help
        Display syntax help.

    --keyword=<KEYWORD>
        Specifies an ADRDSSU DUMP command keyword. Use quotes to pass special
        characters, such as parentheses. Use the --keyword argument repeatedly
        to specify multiple keywords.

    -m, --management-class=<MANAGEMENT_CLASS>
        Specifies the user-desired management class that is to be used
        when creating temporary and target datasets.

    -o, --overwrite
        Overwrite file or dataset destination if it already exists.

    --process-sys1
        Allows ADRDSSU to dump datasets with a high-level qualifier of SYS1.
        This requires READ access to the STGADMIN.ADR.DUMP.PROCESS.SYS profile
        in the FACILITY class. Equivalent to the PROCESS(SYS1) command keyword.

    -Q, --tmphlq=<TEMP_HLQ>
        Use an alternative high-level qualifier (HLQ) for the temporary dataset
        name. This will additionally override the TMPHLQ environment variable.

    -s, --size=<SIZE>
        Specify how large to allocate temporary and target datasets. Valid
        units are: CYL, TRK, K, M, G. Defaults to bytes if no unit provided.

    -S, --storage-class=<STORAGE_CLASS>
        Specifies the user-desired storage class is to be used when
        creating temporary and target datasets.

    -t, --to-volume=<TMPVOL>
        Specifies a particular volume should be used when creating
        temporary and target datasets.

    --terse, --no-terse
        Uses AMATERSE to compress and pack the dump generated by ADRDSSU.
        --no-terse must be used with --dataset. Defaults to --terse.

    -T, --dataset-type=<DATASET_TYPE>
        Specifies the type of dataset used for both the temporary and output
        datasets. This may be one of the following: LARGE, SEQ.
        Defaults to SEQ.

    -v, --verbose
        Prints out verbose command information.

    -V, --volume
        Dump a volume instead of datasets. If a volume is provided along with
        dataset information and -V is not specified, dzip will look for
        provided dataset patterns on the provided volume. Equivalent to
        the FULL command keyword.
```

DESCRIPTION

    dzip uses ADRDSSU and AMATERSE to dump and terse a set of datasets into an
    archive file or dataset denoted by <file>. This file or dataset is used by
    dunzip to restore the datasets.

    Datasets retain the format followed by the first HLQ segment.
    For example, MY.DATASET.FILE restores as <HLQ>.DATASET.FILE.

    If you specify multiple dataset patterns with -e, you must separate them
    with a space or comma (,).

    If the -s argument is not provided, the default allocation size of an
    archive is 25 MB for datasets, and 1 GB for a volume.

DATASET PATTERNS

    The following rules apply to <DATASET-PATTERN>.

    The high-level qualifier (HLQ) must be specified in full.

    Use a single asterisk (`*`) in place of exactly one qualifier.
    You can also use it to indicate to DFSMSdss that only part of a
    qualifier has been specified. For example, just the first, last,
    middle, or first and last parts.

    Using the double asterisk (**) with other qualifiers indicates either the
    nonexistence of leading, trailing, or middle qualifiers, or the fact that
    they play no role in the selection process.

    Two asterisks are the maximum permissible in a qualifier.
    If there are two asterisks in a qualifier, they must be the first and last
    characters.

    A question mark (?) or percent sign (%) matches a single character.

ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES

    TMPHLQ
        Uses an alternative high-level qualifier (HLQ) for the temporary dataset
        name. The HLQ you specify with -Q overrides the TMPHLQ
        environment variable.

EXAMPLES

    Archive datasets that match pattern IBMUSER.MVSCMD.* into file mvscmd.dzp:

        dzip /tmp/mvscmd.dzp IBMUSER.MVSCMD.*

    Archive all datasets in volume ZOSAAS:

        dzip -V /tmp/zosaasvol.dzp ZOSAAS

    Archive datasets that match two patterns in volume MYVOL1 into a dataset:

        dzip -D DS.TO.HOLD.BACKUP "IBMUSER.MVSCMD.* IBMUSER.MYCMD.*" MYVOL1

    Archive datasets that match DATA.** but not DATA.SET.*:

        dzip -e DATA.SET.*  /tmp/mybackup.dzp DATA.**

    Archive datasets into a dataset of type LARGE:

        dzip -T LARGE -D ZOAU.LARGE.DUMP "ZOAU.STUFF.*"

    Archive the SYS1.LINKLIB dataset:

        dzip -D --tolerate --compress -s 300M ZOAU.LINKLIB.DUMP SYS1.LINKLIB

EXIT VALUES

    0       Successful compression.
    4       BGYSC3901E  Invalid option.
            BGYSC3904E  Failed to dump contents for zip file, see RC for mvscmd
                        return code.
                        No source dataset matches the pattern.
            BGYSC3907E  Only one volume can be dumped to a zip file, more were
                        specified.
            BGYSC3908E  Invalid dataset name provided.
            BGYSC3909E  Invalid volume serial provided.
            BGYSC3910E  Invalid size provided.
    8       BGYSC3902E  Unable to create zip file.
            BGYSC3903E  Unable to allocate temporary dataset.
            BGYSC3904E  Failed to dump contents for zip file, see RC for mvscmd
                        return code.
                        Could not compress some or all of the datasets.
            BGYSC3905E  Failed to pack contents for zip file, see RC for mvscmd
                        return code.
            BGYSC3906E  Copy failure.
            BGYSC3911E  Destination zip file already exists.
    16      BGYSC3904E  Failed to dump contents for zip file, see RC for mvscmd
                        return code.
            BGYSC3907I  Out of space error.
    Other   Error, see output messages for details.

